<div class="lesson-page">

    <!-- LESSON -->

    <div class="lesson-card">

        <div class="lesson-label">
            DIGITAL SKILLS • MODULE 6 OF 10
        </div>

        <h1>📽️ Microsoft PowerPoint Basics</h1>

        <p>
            Microsoft PowerPoint is a presentation application used to
            create and display information using slides. It is useful
            for school presentations, meetings, training sessions,
            business proposals, and professional communication.
        </p>


        <h2>🎯 What You Will Learn</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>What Microsoft PowerPoint is</li>
            <li>What slides are</li>
            <li>How to create a presentation</li>
            <li>How to add text and images</li>
            <li>How to use simple presentation design principles</li>
            <li>How to present information clearly</li>
        </ul>


        <h2>📑 What Is PowerPoint?</h2>

        <p>
            PowerPoint is software that allows you to create presentations
            made up of individual slides.
        </p>

        <p>
            Each slide can contain text, pictures, charts, shapes,
            tables, and other visual elements.
        </p>


        <h2>🖥️ Understanding Slides</h2>

        <p>
            A slide is a single page of a PowerPoint presentation.
            Several slides can be combined to create a complete
            presentation.
        </p>

        <div class="example-box">
            <strong>Example presentation:</strong><br><br>
            Slide 1 — Title<br>
            Slide 2 — Introduction<br>
            Slide 3 — Main Information<br>
            Slide 4 — Examples<br>
            Slide 5 — Conclusion
        </div>


        <h2>✍️ Adding Text</h2>

        <p>
            PowerPoint provides text boxes and placeholders where you
            can enter titles, headings, paragraphs, and other information.
        </p>

        <p>
            Keep text short and easy to read. A presentation should
            support what you are saying rather than contain an entire
            speech on every slide.
        </p>


        <h2>🖼️ Adding Images</h2>

        <p>
            Images can help make a presentation easier to understand.
            You can insert appropriate pictures, diagrams, and other
            visual elements into your slides.
        </p>


        <h2>🎨 Presentation Design</h2>

        <p>
            A good presentation should be clear and organized.
            Avoid putting too much information on a single slide.
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>Use clear headings.</li>
            <li>Keep important points easy to find.</li>
            <li>Use readable text.</li>
            <li>Keep your design consistent.</li>
            <li>Use visuals when they help explain the topic.</li>
        </ul>


        <h2>✨ Transitions and Animations</h2>

        <p>
            Transitions control how one slide changes to another.
            Animations control how individual objects on a slide
            appear or move.
        </p>

        <div class="tip-box">
            <strong>💡 Tip:</strong>
            Use animations and transitions carefully. Too many effects
            can distract your audience from the main information.
        </div>


        <h2>📊 Presenting Information</h2>

        <p>
            PowerPoint can be used to present information in a structured
            way. A useful presentation normally has a clear beginning,
            main section, and conclusion.
        </p>

        <div class="example-box">
            <strong>Simple presentation structure:</strong><br><br>
            1. Introduce the topic<br>
            2. Explain the main points<br>
            3. Give examples<br>
            4. Summarize the key ideas
        </div>


        <h2>💼 PowerPoint in the Workplace</h2>

        <p>
            Professionals use PowerPoint for meetings, training,
            project presentations, business proposals, reports,
            and educational sessions.
        </p>


        <h2>📝 Module Summary</h2>

        <p>
            Microsoft PowerPoint helps users communicate information
            through presentations. By understanding slides, text,
            images, design, transitions, and presentation structure,
            you can create clearer and more effective presentations.
        </p>


        <div class="tip-box">
            <strong>🎯 Key Point:</strong>
            A good presentation is clear, organized, readable, and
            focused on helping the audience understand the information.
        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- QUIZ -->

    <div class="quiz-card">

        <h2>🧠 Module 6 Quiz</h2>

        <p>
            Answer all 5 questions. You need at least 70% to pass.
        </p>


        <form id="quizForm">

            <div class="question">

                <p>1. What is Microsoft PowerPoint mainly used for?</p>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q1" value="a">
                    Creating presentations
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q1" value="b">
                    Managing computer hardware
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q1" value="c">
                    Browsing websites only
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q1" value="d">
                    Writing computer code only
                </label>

            </div>


            <div class="question">

                <p>2. What is a slide?</p>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q2" value="a">
                    A single page of a presentation
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q2" value="b">
                    A computer mouse
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q2" value="c">
                    A spreadsheet formula
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q2" value="d">
                    A password
                </label>

            </div>


            <div class="question">

                <p>3. Which can be added to a PowerPoint slide?</p>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q3" value="a">
                    Text and images
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q3" value="b">
                    Only passwords
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q3" value="c">
                    Only audio cables
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q3" value="d">
                    Nothing
                </label>

            </div>


            <div class="question">

                <p>4. What controls how one slide changes to another?</p>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q4" value="a">
                    A spreadsheet
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q4" value="b">
                    A transition
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q4" value="c">
                    A password
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q4" value="d">
                    A folder
                </label>

            </div>


            <div class="question">

                <p>5. Why should you avoid putting too much information on one slide?</p>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q5" value="a">
                    It can make the presentation difficult to understand
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q5" value="b">
                    It makes the computer slower every time
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q5" value="c">
                    It deletes the presentation
                </label>

                <label class="option">
                    <input type="radio" name="q5" value="d">
                    It removes PowerPoint
                </label>

            </div>


            <button
                type="button"
                class="quiz-button"
                onclick="checkQuiz()"
            >
                Submit Quiz
            </button>

        </form>


        <div id="result" class="result"></div>


        <a
            id="nextButton"
            href="{{ url_for('digital_skills_module7') }}"
            class="lesson-button next-button"
        >
            Continue to Module 7 →
        </a>

    </div>


    <!-- NAVIGATION -->

    <div class="lesson-navigation">

        <a
            href="{{ url_for('digital_skills_module5') }}"
            class="lesson-button"
        >
            ← Module 5
        </a>

        <a
            href="{{ url_for('digital_skills') }}"
            class="lesson-button"
        >
            Course Home
        </a>

    </div>

</div>


<script>

function checkQuiz() {

    const answers = {
        q1: "a",
        q2: "a",
        q3: "a",
        q4: "b",
        q5: "a"
    };

    let score = 0;
    let answered = 0;

    for (let question in answers) {

        const selected = document.querySelector(
            'input[name="' + question + '"]:checked'
        );

        if (selected) {

            answered++;

            if (selected.value === answers[question]) {
                score++;
            }
        }
    }


    const result = document.getElementById("result");
    const nextButton = document.getElementById("nextButton");

    result.style.display = "block";


    if (answered < 5) {

        result.className = "result fail";

        result.innerHTML =
            "⚠️ Please answer all 5 questions before submitting.";

        nextButton.style.display = "none";

        return;
    }


    const percentage = (score / 5) * 100;


    if (percentage >= 70) {

        result.className = "result pass";

        result.innerHTML =
            "🎉 You passed! Score: " +
            score +
            "/5 (" +
            percentage +
            "%)";

        nextButton.style.display = "inline-block";

    } else {

        result.className = "result fail";

        result.innerHTML =
            "❌ You did not pass. Score: " +
            score +
            "/5 (" +
            percentage +
            "%). Review the lesson and try again.";

        nextButton.style.display = "none";
    }

}

</script>

{% endblock %}
